## What this changes

Turns the Anduril briefing into the prototype for a reusable, non-narrative reporter report.

The report now presents a fixed analytical sequence:

```text
working proposition and evidence boundary
→ categorical three-by-three orientation
→ canonical multi-lane decision sequence
→ complete evidence-and-gap matrix
→ visible counterweights
→ prioritized reporting queue
→ canonical claim register and public receipt index
```

## Why

The first briefing contract prevented factual drift, but it still left too much of the investigation implicit and used exact x/y coordinates that suggested unsupported measurement precision. A reporter should be able to see the chronology, paper gaps, alternative explanations, and next records moves without reading the whole ledger or accepting a generated narrative.

## Platform contract

- advances the source contract to `reporter-briefing@2` and the compiled manifest to `compiled-reporter-briefing@2`;
- rejects continuous placement and requires exactly three categorical levels per dimension;
- requires one explicit cell per thread and evidence dimension: canonical claims, an open-record target, or `not_applicable`;
- requires canonical event references for the decision sequence;
- requires counterweight groups and an executable reporting queue;
- preserves claim text, status, qualification, dates, receipts, publication history, and approval refusals;
- keeps `graph_effect: none` and `conclusion_generated: false`;
- separates the compiler, HTML renderer, and visual grammar so future reports share the contract without being locked to one subject or stylesheet;
- commits the exact deterministic HTML, manifests, compiled case/index, public catalog, and review queue generated by the same compiler verified in CI.

